        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Peter Brazaitis work for?\n\nAnswer A: Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS)\n  about: Peter Brazaitis\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"According to his biography on the WCS website, Brazaitis worked for the organization, where he contributed to various conservation efforts.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry\n  about: Peter Brazaitis\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"He was on his way to the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry, where he had spent the better part of the last decade unraveling the mysteries of molecular structures and their applications.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a potential Wikipedia entry for Peter Brazaitis:\n\nPeter Brazaitis\n\nPeter Brazaitis is a renowned researcher in the field of wildlife conservation. Career\n\nBrazaitis has had a distinguished career in wildlife conservation, with a significant portion of his work being associated with the Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS). According to his biography on the WCS website, Brazaitis worked for the organization, where he contributed to various conservation efforts. [1]\n\nDuring his tenure at WCS, Brazaitis was involved in several projects focused on protecting and preserving wildlife habitats, as well as researching and developing effective conservation strategies. His work with WCS has had a lasting impact on the field of wildlife conservation, and his expertise has been sought after by organizations and governments around the world. References\n\n[1] Wildlife Conservation Society. (n.d.). Peter Brazaitis.\nDocument 2: As Dr. Peter Brazaitis navigated the winding streets of Prague, the morning sun casting a warm glow on the historic buildings, he couldn't help but feel a sense of excitement and purpose. He was on his way to the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry, where he had spent the better part of the last decade unraveling the mysteries of molecular structures and their applications. As he pushed open the door to the institute, the familiar scent of pipettes and petri dishes enveloped him, transporting him back to the countless late nights spent in the lab, fueled by coffee and curiosity. He exchanged warm greetings with his colleagues, who were already deep in conversation about the latest breakthroughs in synthetic biology. Peter made his way to his own workstation, where a neatly organized array of beakers, test tubes, and microscopes awaited him. He began to prepare for the day's experiments, his hands moving with the precision of a surgeon as he measured out reagents and carefully pipetted them into waiting vessels. As he worked, his mind wandered back to the project that had brought him to the institute in the first place: the development of novel antibiotics to combat the growing threat of resistant bacteria. It was a daunting task, but Peter was undaunted, driven by the knowledge that his work could have a real impact on people's lives. Just then, his colleague, Dr. Eva Nováková, poked her head into the lab, a mischievous glint in her eye. \"Peter, have you seen the latest batch of compounds from the synthesis team?\" she asked, her voice laced with a hint of excitement. Peter's eyes lit up as he followed Eva to the adjacent lab, where rows of gleaming instruments hummed and whirred, processing the latest batch of experimental compounds. The air was thick with the smell of solvents and the promise of discovery. As they pored over the data, Peter couldn't help but feel a sense of pride and belonging. This was where he was meant to be – surrounded by like-minded individuals, driven by a shared passion for discovery and a desire to make a difference. The Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry was more than just a workplace; it was a community, a hub of innovation and creativity that fueled his own research and inspired him to push the boundaries of what was possible.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
